WEBVTT

00:00.620 --> 00:04.850
We're told that for the past
few months, Old Maple Farms

00:04.850 --> 00:08.690
has grown about 1,000 more
apples than their chief rival

00:08.690 --> 00:11.000
in the region, River Orchards.

00:11.000 --> 00:14.180
Due to cold weather this year,
the harvests at both farms

00:14.180 --> 00:15.920
were down by about a third.

00:15.920 --> 00:19.050
However, both farms made up for
some of the shortfall by

00:19.050 --> 00:23.980
purchasing equal quantities
of apples from farms in

00:23.980 --> 00:26.540
neighboring states.

00:26.540 --> 00:28.950
What can you say about
the number of apples

00:28.950 --> 00:30.130
available at each farm?

00:30.130 --> 00:32.800
Does one farm have more than the
other, or do they have the

00:32.800 --> 00:33.670
same amount?

00:33.670 --> 00:35.200
How do I know?

00:35.200 --> 00:37.410
So let's define some
variables here.

00:37.410 --> 00:57.220
Let's let M be equal to number
of apples at Maple Farms. And

00:57.220 --> 00:58.310
then who's the other guy?

00:58.310 --> 00:59.260
River Orchards.

00:59.260 --> 01:03.750
So let's let R be equal
to the number of

01:03.750 --> 01:12.030
apples at River Orchards.

01:12.030 --> 01:14.690
So this first sentence, they
say-- let me do this in a

01:14.690 --> 01:17.540
different color-- they say for
the past few years, Old Maple

01:17.540 --> 01:22.560
Farms has grown about 1,000 more
apples than their chief

01:22.560 --> 01:25.650
rival in the region,
River Orchards.

01:25.650 --> 01:31.500
So we could say, hey, Maple is
approximately Old River, or M

01:31.500 --> 01:34.680
is approximately River
plus 1,000.

01:34.680 --> 01:37.190
Or since we don't know the exact
amount-- it says it's

01:37.190 --> 01:39.940
about 1,000 more, so we don't
know it's exactly 1,000 more--

01:39.940 --> 01:44.080
we can just say that in a normal
year, Old Maple Farms,

01:44.080 --> 01:49.880
which we denote by M, has a
larger amount of apples than

01:49.880 --> 01:51.170
River Orchard.

01:51.170 --> 01:53.750
So in a normal year, M is
greater than R, right?

01:53.750 --> 01:56.620
It has about 1,000 more apples
than Old Maple Farms.

01:56.620 --> 01:59.660
Now, they say due to cold
weather this year-- so let's

01:59.660 --> 02:02.570
talk about this year now-- the
harvests at both farms were

02:02.570 --> 02:03.995
down about a third.

02:07.930 --> 02:09.620
So this isn't a normal year.

02:09.620 --> 02:11.660
Let's talk about what's going
to happen this year.

02:11.660 --> 02:13.700
In this year, each of these
characters are going to be

02:13.700 --> 02:15.280
down by 1/3.

02:15.280 --> 02:18.710
Now if I go down by 1/3, that's
the same thing as being

02:18.710 --> 02:20.510
2/3 of what I was before.

02:20.510 --> 02:21.380
Let me do an example.

02:21.380 --> 02:26.700
If I'm at x, and I take away
1/3x, I'm left with 2/3x.

02:29.400 --> 02:33.800
So going down by 1/3 is the same
thing as multiplying the

02:33.800 --> 02:35.910
quantity by 2/3.

02:35.910 --> 02:38.760
So if we multiply each of these
quantities by 2/3, we

02:38.760 --> 02:40.880
can still hold this inequality,
because we're

02:40.880 --> 02:44.010
doing the same thing to both
sides of this inequality, and

02:44.010 --> 02:46.340
we're multiplying by
a positive number.

02:46.340 --> 02:48.680
If we were multiplying by a
negative number, we would have

02:48.680 --> 02:50.460
to swap the inequality.

02:50.460 --> 02:54.400
So we can multiply both
sides of this by 2/3.

02:54.400 --> 02:59.525
So 2/3 of M is still going to
be greater than 2/3 of R.

02:59.525 --> 03:02.620
And you could even draw that in
a number line if you like.

